e of the  safest cities in america and it's for a  reason  it's because safety is priority for us  and  we continue to work to make sure they  have the tools necessary to keep  themselves safe  to keep them on the cutting edge of  technology  and to make sure that they recognize  that they're valued their life is  important to us and that that their  safety is first as well  was the transition at the head of the  police department satisfactory in your  mind as someone who on the council at  the time  do you think the city has handled that  appropriately so i wasn't on the council  at the time of the transition but i i  believe  based on the information that i have  that it was a good decision and  you know we i meet with the new chief on  a regular basis he does a really good  job  of making sure that i'm aware of what's  happening with his  force the things that are important to  them and the things that maybe are on  the horizon that we need to  really focus on to make sure that we  continue to be safe as we continue to  grow  another issue you brought up is  education one of your uh  stances is trying to separate henderson  with its own school district i believe  there's been a long standing effort to  try and separate ccsd  how would you achieve this goal of  getting henderson its own school  district  well i think we're starting to lay the  groundwork now henderson is the only  city  in the valley that has its own community  education advisory board  and through that board we use 30 percent  of our marijuana funds and 18 percent of  our redevelopment funds to directly fund  henderson public schools and so we've  set the groundwork already for  identifying a funding source and for  ensuring that that our our residents  know that education is top priority  the next thing i've asked our staff to  do and that is already underway is to  looking into  whether or not we can form our own  charter school system  so that we get that already in place and  there's an alternative it'll take a  while to to break up the school district  as you just as you just mentioned  and so we've already done that by  establishing a pre-k program to make  pre-k  available to all children in henderson  we have our own  it's operating at full capacity and it's  it's doing wonderfully well  so i'd like to take that to the next  level and open a charter school  and have that same result as we continue  to work on  and allowing municipalities to separate  or opt out of the school district system  what's wrong with ccsd why separate you  know i think despite  i think every single person at the  school district wants what's best for  our kids i think they all  try their best to make sure that happens  it's just too big there's  it doesn't matter who would be at the  helm it's too big to operate  it doesn't get the the results that we  need to see we just had  a report from the school district at a  recent council meeting  and it continued to reinforce that fact  so we need to do things differently we  need to try alternatives  to make sure that our kids aren't last  or look close to last in the nation in  education  you know i seem to remember even when i  went to school here there were talks of  trying to split up ccsd it's not a new  discussion and not a new issue and  there's always resistance from a variety  of different parties unions legislatures  lawmakers etc  how do you how do you navigate that you  talked about some of the things that are  already in the works but in terms of  actually splitting off  you would have to acknowledge that it's  not as easy as just i would like to  split away  you're right it's a long process and it  would involve getting  a large group of people together all  working in the same direction  since the the pandemic and the kids have  been having the kids home at school home  from school  there's been quite a a raising of  awareness of the need  for different something different i  think there are a lot of people now that  are thinking along those same lines  whereas maybe there wasn't that  groundswell before  and getting those partners together to  work toward the same goal it's going to  take  partnerships with the private sector  with the public sector  and with parents and families you uh  now another issue you brought up  transparency  a long-standing concern from some in the  community is the lack of transparency  whether it's public records whether it's  ability to speak at city council  meetings before and during the pandemic  this isn't a new concern that's been  brought up with the city of henderson  what do you plan to do to address this i  think prior to the pandemic we had  already started efforts to  to approach that we had council  conversations where we would just have  kind of an open forum for residents to  come and meet with their council people  to ask whatever questions  we would bring members of our staff  that were in various departments so that  they could answer questions  and it was just kind of an open forum so  residents were aware of what's happening  what's on the horizon and to voice their  concerns  the mayor did same thing with with her  march on series where  same same thing happened with a specific  topic  that needs to continue as we went  through the pandemic henderson was  i think the only one who had multiple  avenues of participation for our public  meetings when they couldn't come in  person we made sure that they could  participate electronically we made sure  that they could  that they could come on through zoom or  or other mechanisms  and we we adjusted our entire system of  public meetings to make sure that there  were multiple opportunities for people  to chime in on whatever  whatever their concern was a lot of what  you just mentioned though isn't  those aren't earth-shattering ideas  every city had some form of  public comment every municipality  virtually during the pandemic  county commissioners have had county  conversations for a long long time they  go out and have coffee around their  jurisdiction or whatever  so those don't sound like new ideas so  you're describing things that are  already  that some people are criticizing or say  isn't enough  is there anything else that you would  like to address so moving forward  one of the things that that i think  people would like to see is they don't  get to speak with their council people  very often  one-on-one they don't either answer the  phone or they don't  show up so that's something that i have  done throughout my  actually throughout my my tenure at the  city and as as a council person  i'm actually very open door if they  email me i respond  if they call me i respond if they you  know show up to my office i have an open  door and i let them in and i  actually they feel like they are heard  and i get  feedback on that on a regular basis  so that's something i would like to  encourage more of something that we  recently started  was is having roundtable discussions  with our development community  to get feedback on how henderson is  doing and what we could do better to  make sure that the  the process is easier i've been doing  the same thing by reaching out to our  business community and making sure that  what they are experiencing is what  they'd like to see happen that  they are getting the greatest support  possible and i think  it's not earth-shattering but it's  simple to have that one-on-one  interaction and actually have  communication both ways and have  people feel like they've been listened  to goes a long way toward making them  feel like that we're being transparent  one of uh when the discussion of the  dollar loan center as it's called now  uh came up in the deci the votes were  taken to eventually green light the  redevelopment of the pavilion  uh you talk about having people allow  them to have their say  those who were involved the rather vocal  opposition to that project  would suggest they didn't have their say  how do you address  their concerns that i acknowledge the  obviously the arena is being built now  but those are lingering concerns that  were brought up pretty significantly at  that time  so when those people came out i actually  went out and met with them in person i  walked to the site with them  i listened to all of their concerns and  and the vast majority of the concerns  were  traffic potential traffic implications  the visibility that  the the building might impede their  visibility  open space and the availability to to  interact in the open space  and parking so all of those ideas i  brought back  and those were all incorporated with the  building design  so parking has been addressed there have  been more than a thousand parking spots  added to the site without disrupting any  of the existing parking  the building will sit actually nine feet  lower than the previous building  so that that that is not an issue we've  already let out the contract for  the traffic looking at the traffic and  how that might  have impacts we're looking at um  traffic sensing software that will make  sure that the traffic moves smooth  moves smoothly and then we've also made  it so before  the the pavilion not only was it losing  a million  plus dollars a year and was not usable  you know half the year it had just  problem after problem after problem  that that wasn't sustainable so this pl  this new design will have an open space  that has a variety of different issues  or uses that that can be  done on the site and it will be open 24  hours it's not behind a fence like the  previous one it's open all the time and  it'll be for the  for the public to enjoy so i would say  to them that all of their  concerns were definitely heard were  communicated to the design team and were  enacted  but the decision was made during the  pandemic when cuts were being made and  yet  you the project is you know half and  half split with the vegas golden knights  organization  so can the city still afford this  project absolutely  so at the same time as we issued bonds  for this project we  refunded or refinanced some of our  existing bonds which saved  millions of dollars and with that  savings the savings we had  cover the cost of the of the new bonds  and so there was really no change in  what  what has been made and from my  experience  working at the city i know the efforts  that our  finance staff goes through prior to even  coming to us for a bond  and the vetting that has been done to  make sure that worst case scenario  those bonds can be paid and that had all  happened and they had made their  their projections and and evaluated what  the situation was  and how we could cover those bonds  everything looked  great and it has all played out exactly  like they had predicted it would and  and we're in a very good position we'll  now have an 80 million dollar building  that the city only had to pay 40 million  dollars for and we'll have a  professional  sports team that does entertainment uh  for their  you know for their living program  in the space so that we have  professionals doing that as well this is  the best of both  both worlds so the the dollar loan  center  is one of many developments happening in  in henderson there's the area around the  raiders  practice facility there's water street  which obviously as we were talking  before you are heavily involved in  uh there are some major developments on  the horizon how do you hope to or how do  you plan to if you're elected as mayor  continue that growth and and draw more  development into the city  henderson is very fortunate in that we  have people coming to us now and so we  can afford to be very choosy  about who we allow into the city and we  are looking for what's the highest and  best use for the  the limited space that we have available  and we have a very good vetting process  we have an  excellent economic development team a  great redevelopment team  you know everybody from community  development to fire to police are  looking at every project to make sure  that it  crosses all the teas and dots all the  eyes and meets all of the goals that we  have set for the city  to make sure that we're creating jobs  good paying jobs  that have minimal impacts on the  surrounding area and add value to our  city  dan stewart is your opponent right now  for mayor someone you're familiar with  what do you think set yourself apart  from mr stewart  i think our experience is what sets us  apart the most i have  both the the experience 25 years in the  public sector  for my work with the city and in in  redevelopment and then after i retired i  opened my own business became a small  business owner  and worked on the other side of the  table so i could see how  city decisions impacted business  operations on the private side  and how those could be mitigated some  some of the things that that were you  know decided that that  maybe have impacts on the business  community that weren't thought of  and so i have that both sides of the  table experience and besides that i was  a i'm a  long time native of henderson and and i  have that experience from  from all the feedback i get from friends  and family and  people have lived here a long time i  have a very unique background and very  unique experience that i think  makes me the best candidate so what's  your number one priority if you're  elected mayor  number one priority is public safety  without public safety we don't have  anything nobody's going to want to live  here nobody's going to want to send  their child to school  or open a business we've got to make  sure that we have public safety officers  in place  at enough levels to keep our city safe  and are the levels are where they should  be now  we're getting there we're getting there  it it slowed down for a period of time  but in the  in the new budget we have we're adding  more officers and we're continuing to  look down the line to see what's needed  in what areas  michelle romero good luck in your  campaign for mayor of henderson we'll  keep an eye on how the race goes  appreciate it thank you for joining us  thank you very much

